d96a516 exp_07 record: no document is written over evidence that moved
Round-11 finding 3.  The generators protected the canonical JSONs, their
sidecars, the approval blobs and the exp_04 binding report, but not the files
those products declare they were built from.  Archiving an attempt between
admission and publication gives its `args.json` a second name, and `--out`
aimed at either name would have truncated it with a document.  Every input the
admitted products declare is protected now, under the name the product uses and
under the destination's own, and by device and inode -- the check that sees a
hardlink and an archive alike.

`tools.exp07_record.write_outputs`, which publishes the canonical JSON and its
companion, cannot learn the same check: it is inside the calibration receipt's
producer closure and keeps its reviewed bytes.  The README says so, and says
what protects the producers instead: the documented order publishes everything
before anything is archived.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>


 3 files changed, 40 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
fdac647 exp_07 record: `final` must be the promotion of the attempt being bound
Round-11 finding 1.  The approval pins each checkpoint through the arm's `final`
and the binder matched that pin to the attempt's own output by inode, but an
inode names a file, not a directory: with a hardlink of the checkpoint in
another directory, `final` repointed there -- or replaced by an ordinary
directory holding that link -- was accepted, and collection reproduced the
report unchanged.  The arm's `final` is now required to be a symlink naming this
very attempt, which is the directory check exp_05's record already makes.

It is asked after the arm is identified, so a `final` naming another attempt
still fails as "not exactly one approved arm", and it is asked about the
attempt's published name, so an archived attempt or arm answers it through the
directory symlink left behind.

